 ### [Lighting-Specific Compositing](5940-lighting-specific-compositing.md)
-Lighting-Specific Compositing is a family of volume rendering techniques that integrate illumination models (such as in-scattering, Phong shading, or boundary enhancement) directly into the compositing loop, rather than treating lighting as a post-processing step or a simple per-sample color multiplication. It solves the problem of physically inaccurate or visually flat rendering by allowing light transport effects to influence the accumulation of opacity and color at the sample level. It differs from standard direct volume ray casting by modifying the color and opacity terms ($c_i, \alpha_i$) before they enter the front-to-back accumulator, and from image-level compositing by preserving depth coherence for complex lighting interactions like occlusion and shadowing. See [Lighting-Specific Compositing](5940-lighting-specific-compositing.md).
